// The arena: a bump pointer, and free lists for the list blocks the compiler
// proves unreachable (more/pool.id).
//
// docs/SPEC.md 6 says `alloc` is a bump pointer and that nothing is released
// before the process exits. A kernel is the case where that stops being a
// compromise -- there is no exit -- so a list freed by the compiler's escape
// analysis goes onto a free list here, and so does a string it frees. The flat store
// still only bumps. Strings take classes of their own (lang/str/free/give.id).
//
// The pointer lives in the first eight bytes of the arena rather than in an
// exported global, because only a global's declaring function may assign it
// and there is no function the runtime could hang that on. Reading zero there
// means nobody has allocated yet.
//
// The next 640 bytes are the heads of the free lists, 80 size classes (lists 0-35, strings 36 up).
// They live here for the same reason the pointer does, and they are reserved on
// the first allocation rather than declared anywhere: a table at a known offset
// needs no initialiser to be called, which matters because nothing in a
// freestanding program is guaranteed to run before the first list is made. A
// string needs no such table: it carries its own length (lang/str/core.id).

arena_top(word a) {
  word p = ld64(a);
  if(p == 0) {
    p = arena_open(a);
  }
} return word p;
given store_setup(64) given poke64((import tsb), 4096) ((import tsb)):(4096)
given store_setup(704) given store_near given store_far given poke64((import tsp), 99) ((import tsb)):((import tso)) then peek64((import tsp)):(0)

// First use: the free-list heads are zeroed, because whatever the firmware
// left above the kernel image would otherwise read as a block to reuse.
arena_open(word a) {
  pool_wipe(a + 8, 0);
  word ret_w = a + 648;
} return word ret_w;
